================================================================================
BUNDLED QUEST LOG ARTWORK
================================================================================

The three textures under media/QuestLog/ draw the standalone two-page Quest Log.
They belong to the Dragonflight visual language of DragonflightUI (Guzruul;
reforged by Stormhand), which ships no license text of its own and part of whose
art derives from Blizzard UI assets. They were copied unchanged from unrealUI's
modern-wow theme media so that the same window is drawn with or without that
addon installed.

These files are not represented as original UnrealQuest artwork by the MIT
license above, and their inclusion is not a license grant. See
media/QuestLog/ATTRIBUTION.md, which must stay with them.

Until 0.3.6 this folder instead carried the parchment textures of Extended
QuestLog 3.6.1, Copyright (c) 2006 Daniel Rehn. They are no longer shipped or
drawn.


================================================================================
AREA CONTOUR TECHNIQUE ATTRIBUTION
================================================================================

The two normal/neutral textures under media/ named
AreaContoursForeverDirect.tga and AreaContoursForeverDirectNeutral.tga are
generated by UnrealQuest. Their 16-mask layout and signed-distance technique
were adapted from Questline's generated quest-area contour atlas. Questline's
distribution includes no license text for that atlas; its README credits
pfQuest material under MIT. The normal atlas also reproduces the colour and
alpha profile of Blizzard's `UI-QuestBlob-Inside` and
`UI-QuestBlob-Outside` brushes exported with Forever FrameXML; the source PNGs
are not bundled. See media/AREA-CONTOURS-ATTRIBUTION.md for details.


================================================================================
QUEST POI NUMBER TEXTURE ATTRIBUTION
================================================================================

media/QuestPOINumbers.tga is an imported, precomposed derivative of Blizzard's
Interface/WorldMap/UI-QuestPoi-NumberIcons texture exported with Forever
FrameXML. It combines the source texture's separate circle and number regions
into one region per number and state for this client's single-texture map-pin
contract. The source PNG is not bundled. See
media/QUEST-POI-NUMBERS-ATTRIBUTION.md for details.


================================================================================
BUNDLED WORLD DATA
================================================================================

The files under Database/ are not original to this project. They are quest,
creature, object, item and zone records describing the game world.

Origin
------

The data originates from the world database of VMaNGOS, the open-source
1.12 server project, and was packaged into Lua tables by pfQuest, a quest
helper addon by Eric Mauser (Shagu). This project takes pfQuest's packaged
tables as its starting point.

What this project changed
-------------------------

The pfQuest database was reduced to the records a quest helper actually needs,
and nothing else:

  * Vanilla content only. All TBC data removed.
  * English only. All other localizations removed.
  * Quest profession and skill restriction fields removed, and the profession
    tables dropped entirely.
  * Vendor item-source relations removed, along with vendor-only NPCs.
  * Only quest-reachable creatures, objects, items, reference-loot groups,
    quest item-use targets and area triggers retained.
  * Dangling references with no matching source record removed.
  * The tables were renamed onto this addon's own global so the data carries no
    dependency on pfQuest runtime code.

Zone, minimap and English zone-name tables are kept intact, being small
coordinate-support tables.

The data tables, the `media/QuestDot.tga` minimap marker, the
`media/icons/*.tga` service icons and the `media/icons/herbs/*.tga` and
`media/icons/mines/*.tga` gathering-node icons are reused under the license
below. `Data/NodeIcons.lua` likewise reuses pfQuest's assignment of those node
icons to game objects, resolved to object IDs at build time.
`Data/QuestMatch.lua` also uses pfQuest's quest-text token vocabulary and
substitution semantics as prior art; it does not copy pfQuest's first-best-
candidate identity policy.

